{"id": "section-body-13917", "title": "Ocular Trachoma", "chapterId": "chapter-fourteen", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "EYE DISEASES AND REFRACTIVE ERRORS", "Major Blinding Eye Diseases", "Ocular Trachoma"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-13917", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Ocular Trachoma", "runs": [{"text": "Ocular"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Trachoma"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "1312"}}, {"id": "body-13918", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "It has five grades according to WHO which are grouped into two (Active Trachoma and Cicatricial Trachoma). Active trachoma usually happened in children below age of 9 years as is an acute conjunctivitis caused by infection with Chlamydia trachomatis (bacteria). The Cicatricial trachoma (TS, TT and CO) is one of the commonest causes of corneal blindness worldwide. There is an inward turning of the eye lashes which rub against the cornea and lead to corneal opacity which is the culprit for visual impairment and/or blindness.", "runs": [{"text": "It"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "has"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "five"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "grades"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "according"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "WHO"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "which"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "are"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "grouped"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "into"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "two"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(Active"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Trachoma"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Cicatricial"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Trachoma)."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Active"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "trachoma usually"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "happened"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "children"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "below"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "age"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "9"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "yea